  "id": "DrugBank_DB06824 - MONDO_5086",
  "verbalization": "Triethylenetetramine is implicated in the pathogenesis of Renal Cell Carcinoma (RCC) through a series of interconnected indications. The drug is linked to RCC via the indication of Tivozanib and Axitinib, both of which are approved for the treatment of RCC. These medications are known to inhibit vascular endothelial growth factor (VEGF) signaling, a key pathway in RCC progression. The presence of Triethylenetetramine as an indication for RCC suggests a potential role in the disease process, though the exact mechanism remains to be elucidated. The evidence is supported by a consistent pattern of drug–disease relationships, with Triethylenetetramine acting as a potential modulator in RCC through a mechanism that may involve VEGF inhibition or other pathways yet to be fully characterized. The integration of these indications highlights the possibility that Triethylenetetramine could be a candidate for repurposing in RCC, provided further mechanistic validation is obtained.",
